Now I understand the structure. The task: in Restore, each request's `createdAt` (submission time) must satisfy:
- `createdAt >= policy.startsAt` (not before; exactly at start is OK)
- `createdAt < policy.endsAt` (strictly before; exactly at end rejected)
- `createdAt < session.expiresAt` (strictly before; exactly at expiry rejected)

This applies to all requests (direct reserve and approval-path), all states (reserved, settled, cancelled, rejected, expired, reservation-expired, pending). Based on backup's recorded times, not restore time. Rejected ledger entries (LedgerRejection without associated request) are exempt — they're just ledger entries, not requests, so nothing to do there.

Where to add: in `validateRequestTimingAndState`, since it has access to r, p, sess. Add a check like `validateSubmissionTiming(r, p, sess)`:

```go
func validateSubmissionTiming(r *request, p *policy, sess *session) error {
    if r.createdAt.Before(p.startsAt) {
        return fmt.Errorf("created_at %v is before policy %q window start %v", ...)
    }
    if !r.createdAt.Before(p.endsAt) {
        return fmt.Errorf("created_at %v is not strictly before policy %q window end %v", ...)
    }
    if !r.createdAt.Before(sess.expiresAt) {
        return fmt.Errorf("created_at %v is not strictly before session %q expiry %v", ...)
    }
    return nil
}
```

Error message must identify usage account, request ID, and whether policy window or session expiry was violated. The caller wraps with `%w: request %q/%q: %v` including account and request IDs. So the inner error should mention policy window vs session expiry. Good.

Where to call: in `validateRequestTimingAndState` after createdAt zero check — applies to all states. Note createdAt.IsZero() check already exists; place after it.

